How to generate the PDF rendering instructions in a .form file:

== STEP 1: MARKUP

Open the PDF file in an editor and add dabs of color in each of the fields.
They don't have to fill the field or be precisely placed; they just need to be
fully inside the field.  Specifically:

- For text fields that are bounded in black, add a dab of pure red (#FF0000FF).
  If the field has a text label inside it, add the dab to the right of the
  label if you want the field contents to appear to its right, or below the
  label if you want the field contents to appear below it.
- For checkbox fields, add a dab of pure green (#00FF00FF) inside the box.
- For radio button fields, add a dab of pure blue (#0000FFFF) inside the
  circle.
- For areas that do need precise sizing or placement, add a rectangle of pure
  magenta (#FF00FFFF) in the exact space and size desired.  This can be useful
  for:
  - The printed page number at the bottom of the form, so it can be overlaid.
  - Text fields that are not bounded in black.

Save the resulting file as PNG images, one per page.  600 dpi is recommended.

== STEP 2: DISTRIBUTE PDF RENDER LINES

For each page, run the make-field-boxes command on the PNG file, and redirect
the result to a temp file.  (Use the -p flag to set the page number for all but
the first page.)  This generates the PDF rendering lines corresponding to the
dabs of color.  In a text editor, transfer the rendering lines to the proper
fields in the .form file.

The "pdf box" lines for the page numbers go in an otherwise anonymous field,
i.e., under a "field -" line.

== STEP 3: VERIFY PDF RENDER LINES

Run the show-field-boxes command on the .form file.  It will generate and open
a PDF with each of the fields outlined is pastel red.  Make sure they are all 
correct; adjust the PDF render lines as needed.

Sometimes multiple text fields on adjacent lines should be aligned horizontally.
Make the necessary changes to the coordinates on the "pdf text" lines and
re-run show-field-boxes to verify.

== STEP 4: CUSTOMIZE THE RENDERING

Several customizations are available and/or needed:
- For text fields placed with a magenta rectangle, change the pdf line from
  "pdf box" to "pdf text".
- To overlay the printed page number areas, add "F FFFFFF" (i.e., "fill with
  white") to their "pdf box" lines.
- For text fields that need unusual alignment within the field, add an "A"
  clause to the "pdf text" line.  Other text styling changes can also be made.
- For radio buttons, change each "pdf circle" to "pdf if «tag»=«value» then
  circle" so that the circle is shown only for the appropriate value.
- The radius of the radio button circles can be adjusted if it's not
  aesthetically pleasing.

== STEP 5: ADD BASELINES

When text field(s) occur on the same line as text label(s), it looks best for
the fields and labels to have the same baseline.  To achieve this, run the 
pdf-baselines command on the .pdf file, redirecting the result to a temp file.
(If the PDF has multiple pages, run it once for each page, with the -p pagenum
flag.)  The resulting text file gives the baselines for each static text string
in the PDF.  For each text field that needs a baseline, find the appropriate
baseline value in the pdf-baselines output, and add it to that field's "pdf
text" line as a "BL" clause.  Re-run show-field-boxes to check proper placement.

== STEP 6: CLEANUP

Remove the temp files and the *.boxes.pdf files generated by show-field-boxes.
